Hi Mario, Mika and Alex,=0A=
=0A=
I tested the Kensington SD5000T5 dock again by using Ubuntu 26.04.1 Live=0A=
with kernel 7.0.0-30-generic and thunderbolt.dyndbg=3D+p. The dock was=0A=
disconnected at boot, and I did not run lspci before the hotplug=0A=
log and power states had been saved.=0A=
=0A=
In this Ubuntu run the PCIe/USB enumeration worked without any workaround:=
=0A=
=0A=
  426.716: USB4 dock connection detected (host port 0:2, link up)=0A=
  426.753: Kensington SD5000T5 discovered=0A=
  428.410: PCIe Down path activation complete=0A=
  428.411: PCIe Up path activation complete=0A=
  429.025: pcieport 0000:00:04.1 reports Card present / Link Up=0A=
  429.026: downstream 0000:09:00.0 (8086:5786) enumerated=0A=
=0A=
After the dock had connected, but *before* running lspci, the sysfs=0A=
states were:=0A=
=0A=
  0000:00:04.1  power/control=3Dauto; runtime_status=3Dactive; power_state=
=3DD0=0A=
  0000:09:00.0  present=0A=
  0000:6b:00.6  runtime_status=3Dactive; power_state=3DD0=0A=
=0A=
The downstream USB devices and dock Ethernet also enumerated. I then=0A=
ran lspci once: 0000:00:04.1 remained active/D0, 0000:09:00.0 remained=0A=
present. Thus this run cannot show an lspci-triggered wakeup or a =0A=
resulting PME event: it was already working.=0A=
In my previous failing CachyOS runs, invoking lspci could wake 00:04.1=0A=
and make 09:00.0 appear. I do not see an explicit PME delivery message=0A=
at hotplug in the Ubuntu dmesg.=0A=
=0A=
The external display did still flicker. The Ubuntu log includes one=0A=
DP tunnel DPRX read timeout at ~431.188s, followed by a completed=0A=
capability read at ~431.239s, I do not know if it's related.=0A=
=0A=
Attached are the full Ubuntu dmesg after/before hotplug and the state files=
.=0A=
Unrelated USB serial numbers and network interface MAC addresses are redact=
ed.=0A=
=0A=
Thanks,=0A=
Freeman=0A=
